---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import NnMasthead from '../../components/NnMasthead.astro';
import NnPreviousYears from '../../components/NnPreviousYears.astro';
import NnRaceSummary from '../../components/NnRaceSummary.astro';
import race from '../../content/race.json';

// The Nightingale Nightmare event theme — the campaign look, and nothing the campaign
// knows that this site does not. See packages/shared/styles/nn-theme.css.
import '@src/shared/styles/nn-theme.css';

// **The race, not one running of it — and this page never names a year.**
//
// A race is the recurring thing; an event is one running of it in one year. This page is the
// first; `/nn/2026/` is the second. That distinction is why publishing 2027 is a row in
// `entries.events` and a new directory of content pages, with **no edit to this file at all**.
//
// **Every link from here to a year page is painted by the Worker**, from
// `entries.current_entry_state('nn')`. There is no `href="/nn/2026/"` anywhere below, and
// there must never be one: it would be the line somebody forgot in 2027, and it would be
// wrong quietly rather than loudly. See `worker/nn-entry.ts`'s `renderNnRaceView`.
//
// ## Which of `race.json`'s keys this page may use, and why the split is not in that file yet
//
// `race.json` describes **the 2026 running**, and it always has: the date, the start time,
// race HQ, the entry fee, the entry window and the ARC permit are all facts about one running
// of this race. **None of them appears on this page any more.** What is left is what is true
// of the race itself — its name, its distance, its field size, and the address to write to.
//
// **The file is not split, and that is deliberate rather than unfinished.** Separating it into
// a race file and a year file is a content change of its own, with its own review, and doing
// it inside a route reorganisation would put two unrelated diffs in one commit. Until then the
// rule is this comment plus the tests: `site.spec.ts` asserts the date and race HQ are on the
// year page and **not** here.
//
// **This page states no date, and that is the one thing somebody may notice is missing.** It
// cannot use `race.json`'s, because that is 2026's; it could be painted from `entry_state()`,
// which returns the event date — but rendering "Sunday 1 November 2026" from a `CivilDate`
// means a second date formatter in a repository whose whole timezone discipline is that there
// is exactly one. The date is one tap away, on the running the block below links to, and that
// is the cheaper side of the trade. **Recorded as a gap rather than a decision to leave
// closed** — see ADR-011.
//
// See docs/architecture/principles.md#stop-and-ask

// One address, and it is a path rather than a subdomain — so at the Squarespace cutover
// the hostname changes and this page does not move.
const canonical = 'https://new.southvillerunningclub.co.uk/nn/';
---

{
  /* **The title names the race and not what the page currently offers.** It read
  "— register your interest", which is one of the two states this page has and became wrong
  the moment the other one was possible. A title is also what a bookmark and a search result
  are called, and both outlive a window. */
}
<Base
  title="Nightingale Nightmare"
  description="Nightingale Nightmare — a tough off-road 10 km in fancy dress, put on by Southville Running Club."
  canonical={canonical}
  theme="nn"
>
  <NnMasthead slot="masthead" />

  <header class="nn-hero">
    <div class="nn-wrap">
      {
        /* **"presents…" opens the hero now that the wordmark has moved to the masthead.**
        The sentence it belongs to still reads the same way down the page — "Southville
        Running Club" in the header, then "presents…", then the title — which is what
        somebody seeing it gets and, because the header is the first thing in the DOM, what
        somebody hearing it gets too. */
      }
      <p class="nn-presents">presents…</p>

      <h1>{race.name}</h1>

      {
        /* **The three answers somebody wants before they read a sentence.** A real list,
        because that is what it is, with the bullets taken off — a screen reader still says
        "list, three items" and gets the same summary a sighted reader gets from the row.

        The mockup has a fourth item here, a live "238 of 250 places remaining" bar. That is
        demo data, and the database could answer it now — but a number that is wrong within a
        day of anybody entering, cached at the edge and read off a poster-shaped page, is a
        claim the club would have to keep true. How big the race is does not change, so that
        is what this says. Somebody who tries to enter a full race is told plainly, on the
        form, at the moment it matters. */
      }
      <ul class="nn-facts">
        <li>{race.distance}</li>
        <li>{race.places} places</li>
        <li>Fancy dress encouraged</li>
      </ul>

      {
        /* **The lede lost one clause and it was the year-specific one.** It used to say "the
        date is settled", which is a statement about 2026 on a page about the race. That
        sentence is on the year page now, where it is true of the thing being described. */
      }
      <p class="lede">
        The course has not got any kinder. Leave your name and the club will email you
        once, when entries open.
      </p>

      {
        /* **The loud button is the one this site can actually honour**, and which that is
        depends on the day. It ships as "Register your interest", pointing at the form on this
        page; when `entries.events` says entries are open the Worker repoints it at the entry
        form on the year page and relabels it. That is one row edit and no deploy, which is the
        property the event table exists for — and it is why the label and the target are
        painted on rather than written in.

        **There is no second button here any more.** It used to be "Race-day plan", pointing
        straight at `/nn/race-day/`; that page now lives under the year, and a year in this
        page's markup is exactly what this page must not hold. The links to this year's running
        are in the block below, where the Worker fills them in. */
      }
      <div class="nn-herobtns">
        <a class="nn-cta" href="#register" data-nn-cta>Register your interest</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="nn-wrap">
    {
      /* **The race-facts list moved to the year page in one piece.** Every row in it — the
      date, the start time, race HQ, the entry fee, when entries open, the ARC permit — is a
      fact about *one running*, and half of them are `null` for 2026 specifically. A page about
      the race would have been listing next year's unknowns as this year's.

      What is left of it here is the row of three in the hero: the distance, the field size and
      fancy dress, which are what this race is however it is run. */
    }
    {
      /* **A summary that ends in a link, not a second copy of the course page.** Four things
      somebody should know before they leave their name — and the same four somebody should
      know before they enter, which is why they are one component rather than two copies. See
      `NnRaceSummary.astro`.

      The mockup has a fifth line here — "Minimum age 18 on race day" — which is inferred from
      the prize categories rather than stated as a rule anywhere. It is not here, and it is not
      softened into "18+ probably" either. */
    }
    <div class="nn-card nn-prose">
      <h2>Know what you are in for</h2>

      <NnRaceSummary />

      <p>
        <a href="/nn/course/">The course and terrain, in full</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    {
      /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       * The year panel — when is it, and can I enter
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       * **A panel, not a link.** This is the front door's answer to the two questions
       * somebody arriving from a shared link actually has, in the order they have them: the
       * date first and largest, then whether they can enter yet.
       *
       * **Ships hidden and empty; the Worker fills it in** from
       * `entries.current_entry_state('nn')`, so 2027 is a row in `entries.events` rather than
       * an edit to this file. There is no `href="/nn/2026/"` anywhere below and there must
       * never be one — see the note at the head of this file.
       *
       * **A database this Worker cannot reach paints nothing**, so the panel stays hidden and
       * the page is the summary card and the interest form. One fewer door in the front door,
       * which is the right side of that trade against a link to a year nobody confirmed.
       *
       * ## Two states, one shape
       *
       * The layout does not move when entries open, so nobody has to relearn the page at the
       * one moment they are trying to do something. Only two things change: the action goes
       * from a quiet outline to the filled button every other primary control on this site
       * uses, and the fee line appears under it. **The difference in prominence is the
       * message** — there is deliberately no badge and no banner saying "open", because the
       * button already says it and a page that said it twice would be shouting.
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    }

    <section
      class="nn-card nn-panel-year"
      data-nn-panel
      hidden
      aria-labelledby="next-race"
    >
      <p class="nn-panel-label" id="next-race">The next race</p>

      {
        /* **The biggest thing in the panel, at 24px.** Somebody arriving from a shared link
        asks "when is it" first, and everything else here is smaller than this on purpose.

        Painted rather than read from `race.json`: that file's date belongs to the 2026
        running, and this page is about the race. The value comes through `packages/shared`'s
        one date formatter — see `formatEventDate`. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-date" data-nn-panel-date></p>

      <p class="nn-panel-facts">
        <span data-nn-panel-time></span> · {race.distance} · {race.places} places
      </p>

      <hr class="nn-panel-rule" />

      {
        /* **One control in two weights**, not two controls with one hidden. A second button
        revealed beside a hidden one is two things to keep in step and two places for a label
        to go stale; the Worker swaps this one's class and its destination. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-actions">
        <a class="nn-ghost" href="" data-nn-panel-action data-nn-panel-link="year">
          The race this year
        </a>
      </p>

      {
        /* **Before entries open, and it names no month.** "Entries open in September" would be
        a claim about a date nobody has confirmed — the entry open and close times are on this
        repository's unconfirmed list and may not appear anywhere. What is true without
        confirmation is that they are not open and that the club will say when they are.

        When the committee settles the opening time, the honest home for it is
        `entries.events.entries_open_at` — which `entry_state()` deliberately does not return
        yet, for exactly this reason — and this line becomes painted like the date above it. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-note" data-nn-panel-shut>
        Entries are not open yet. Leave your name and the club will email you the day they
        are.
      </p>

      {
        /* The fee line, and it is the database's numbers or nothing. `entries.fees` is the
        only place a price exists, and this is painted from the same read the rest of the
        panel is. */
      }
      <p class="nn-panel-note" data-nn-panel-open hidden>
        <span data-nn-panel-fees></span>
      </p>

      {
        /* The rest of this year's running, inside the panel because that is what it is about.
        Both are painted. */
      }
      <ul class="nn-panel-links">
        <li><a href="" data-nn-panel-link="race-day">Race day</a></li>
        <li><a href="" data-nn-panel-link="spectators">Watching the race</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <NnPreviousYears />

    <div data-nn-interest>
      <h2 id="register">Register your interest</h2>

      {
        /* The acknowledgement, the failure notice and the error summary are all served hidden
      and revealed by the Worker — see worker/nn-signup.ts. Doing it this way means there is
      exactly one copy of this page, in dist/, and the Worker paints an outcome onto it
      rather than holding a second template that can drift from this one.

      Each carries `tabindex="-1"` so the Worker can add `autofocus` and move focus to it.
      That is how focus moves **with JavaScript disabled**, which is the primary path here
      rather than a fallback.

      All three are white cards in the event theme, because all three are read rather than
      glanced at, and none of them lets colour carry the meaning: each says in words what it
      is. */
      }

      <div
        class="notice notice-ok nn-card"
        data-signup-ack
        hidden
        tabindex="-1"
        role="status"
      >
        {
          /* The mockup's confirmation tick. Decorative, and hidden from assistive technology
        deliberately — this element is a live region, and what should be announced is the
        sentence below, not the word "tick". */
        }
        <span class="nn-tick" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false"
            ><path d="M4 12.5 10 18 20 6"></path></svg
          >
        </span>
        <p><strong>Thank you — your interest is registered.</strong></p>
        <p>
          The club will email you once, when entries open. That is the only thing this
          list is used for.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="notice notice-bad nn-card"
        data-signup-unavailable
        hidden
        tabindex="-1"
        role="alert"
      >
        <p><strong>Sorry — that could not be saved just now.</strong></p>
        <p>
          Nothing has been recorded, and nothing you typed has been lost. Please press
          "Register my interest" again in a moment.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="notice notice-bad nn-card"
        data-signup-summary
        hidden
        tabindex="-1"
        role="alert"
      >
        <h3>There is a problem</h3>
        <ul class="summary-list">
          <li data-signup-summary-item="name" hidden>
            <a data-signup-summary-link="name" href="#signup-name"></a>
          </li>
          <li data-signup-summary-item="email" hidden>
            <a data-signup-summary-link="email" href="#signup-email"></a>
          </li>
          <li data-signup-summary-item="consent" hidden>
            <a data-signup-summary-link="consent" href="#signup-consent"></a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      {
        /* **Above the form, not below it.** What happens to a name and an email address is
      worth knowing before typing them rather than after, and somebody who wants to check
      first should not have to scroll past the thing they are deciding about to find out. */
      }

      <p class="signup-privacy">
        The club holds your name and email address, and uses them for nothing but this one
        email. <a href="/nn/privacy/">What the club does with this</a>, including how to
        have it removed.
      </p>

      {
        /* A real form, posting to a real address. No JavaScript is involved in submitting it,
      validating it, or rendering what comes back — runners are on phones, on poor signal,
      sometimes in bright sunlight with cold hands.

      `action` carries the trailing slash Astro is configured to insist on, so a submission
      never arrives at a second address for the same page. */
      }

      {
        /* **The hidden `form` field is gone with the ambiguity that needed it.** Both forms
        used to be on this page and a field in the body was what told them apart. They are on
        two pages now — interest here, entry on the year page — so the address a submission
        arrives at is the discriminator, and it is one nobody can mistype into the wrong
        handler. */
      }
      <form class="signup nn-card" method="post" action="/nn/">
        <div class="field">
          <label class="field-label" for="signup-name">Your name</label>
          <p class="field-error" id="signup-name-error" data-signup-error="name" hidden>
          </p>
          <input
            class="field-input"
            id="signup-name"
            name="name"
            type="text"
            maxlength="100"
            autocomplete="name"
            required
            aria-describedby="signup-name-error"
            data-signup-value="name"
          />
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label class="field-label" for="signup-email">Email address</label>
          <p class="field-hint" id="signup-email-hint">
            The club will email you once, when entries open.
          </p>
          <p class="field-error" id="signup-email-error" data-signup-error="email" hidden>
          </p>
          <input
            class="field-input"
            id="signup-email"
            name="email"
            type="email"
            maxlength="254"
            autocomplete="email"
            spellcheck="false"
            required
            aria-describedby="signup-email-hint signup-email-error"
            data-signup-value="email"
          />
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <p
            class="field-error"
            id="signup-consent-error"
            data-signup-error="consent"
            hidden
          >
          </p>
          <div class="field-check">
            {
              /* **`required` is one half of the consent decision.** Consent is currently
            required to submit; the other half is `z.literal(true)` in
            packages/shared/src/nn-signup.ts. If the committee decides an unconsented
            submission should be stored instead, remove `required` here and change that to
            `z.boolean()`. No migration is involved — the policy's `with check` is
            deliberately silent on consent. */
            }
            <input
              class="field-checkbox"
              id="signup-consent"
              name="consent"
              type="checkbox"
              required
              aria-describedby="signup-consent-error"
              data-signup-value="consent"
            />
            <label class="field-label" for="signup-consent">
              Yes, email me when entries open for this race.
            </label>
          </div>
        </div>

        <button class="button" type="submit">Register my interest</button>
      </form>
    </div>

    <h2>What this page proves</h2>

    <p>
      The timestamp below was fetched from Postgres by the Worker while it served this
      request. It is rendered server-side, so it is here with JavaScript disabled. If it
      reads as an error, the connection is genuinely broken and that is the useful answer.
    </p>

    <dl class="status">
      <dt>Database time, Europe/London</dt>
      <dd>
        {
          /* The Worker fills this in while serving the request — see worker/index.ts. The
          `datetime` attribute carries UTC and the text carries Europe/London, which is
          the storage-and-display rule expressed in one element. */
        }
        <time data-health="pending">Not fetched — the Worker did not run.</time>
      </dd>

      <dt>Pipeline check</dt>
      <dd>
        {
          /* A second, independent database round trip — intake.ping(), added after the
          skeleton's first deploy to prove a *new* migration reaches this page the same
          way the one above already did. */
        }
        <span data-pipeline-check="pending">Not fetched — the Worker did not run.</span>
      </dd>

      <dt>Served by</dt>
      <dd>Cloudflare Workers, static assets plus one handler</dd>

      <dt>Application</dt>
      <dd>apps/main</dd>
    </dl>

    <footer>
      {
        /* **The "to be confirmed" sentence went with the facts list it was about.** There is
        nothing above reading it any more — the rows that did are on the year page, which
        carries the sentence. A footer explaining a marker the page does not use is the sort of
        thing a reader trusts and then cannot find. */
      }
      <p>
        Southville Running Club. This page is about the race itself; each year's running
        has its own, linked above.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions? <a href={`mailto:${race.contact}`}>{race.contact}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/nn/privacy/">What the club does with your details</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>

  {
    /* The club's own artwork, at the foot of the page. **Empty on purpose** — it is a CSS
    background, not an `<img>`, because it is decoration that states nothing and because an
    image with intrinsic dimensions is briefly 1080 pixels wide before the stylesheet lands.
    See the artwork section of nn-theme.css. */
  }
  <div class="nn-hands" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</Base>
